{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 39, :comments ["<b>Behold, of the fatness of the earth.</b> You will not find in the blessing of Esav any mention of the Name, may He be exalted, whereas in the blessing of Yaakov he did mention it — 'and may El give you' — and this indicates that He gave Esav fatness and dew not as a gift from Elohim as in the case of Yaakov, but by way of the courses of the stars and constellations to which the seed of Esav is subjected, and He granted them to him only for a time. This is the sense of 'your dwelling' (מושבך), that is, while you dwell in the land — in the language said concerning the children of Qein (Numbers 24:21): 'איתן מושבך' — alluding in both of them to destruction at their end. And he used concerning him the word 'behold' (הנה), which is the language of appointment for a time; and so we find concerning Yishma'el (Genesis 17:20): 'הנה ברכתי אותו' — and this shows that their blessing is only in matters of this world. And furthermore, the blessing of Esav applies only when Yisra'el do not do the will of the Omnipresent, for the blessings were conditional, like all the promises of the Torah, which are conditional. Concerning this it was said, 'and may El give you' — on condition: if you are worthy, He gives, and if you are not worthy, He does not give, but they belong to Esav. This is what it says: 'והיה כאשר תריד ופרקת עלו מעל צוארך' — and if so, the blessings are not prepared for Esav in this world except when Yisra'el are not worthy."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 27, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:15:40.550631346Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}